{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["He said: <b>\"These are the words that Moses spoke to all Israel\"</b> — concerning the commandments that he will mention throughout the entire book, beginning with the Ten Commandments in the parashah of Va'etchanan, as it says (Deuteronomy 1:5), \"Moses undertook (הוֹאִיל) to expound this Torah, saying,\" for he will speak about the Torah, and there is missing from the verse \"YHWH our Elohim spoke to us\" (Deuteronomy 1:6) the word for \"speaking\" — and its parallel is \"for Elohim has made me forget (נַשַּׁנִי) all my toil, for Elohim has made me fruitful (הִפְרַנִי)\" (Genesis 41:51–52), where the meaning is \"said.\" And the full measure of these verses is: \"These are the commandments that Moses spoke to all Israel across the Jordan in the fortieth year of their going out from Egypt, on the first day of the eleventh month, according to all that YHWH commanded him to them\" — and this was after striking Sihon and Og in the land of Moab; there Moses undertook to expound this Torah to them, saying, and he said: \"YHWH our Elohim spoke to us at Horeb, saying: You have dwelt long enough...\" and so on. And the reason is that when Moses undertook to expound the commandments to them, he said to them at the beginning of his words: \"YHWH our Elohim spoke to us at Horeb\" — after He had given us the Ten Commandments — \"that we conquer the land immediately and cross the Jordan,\" but your sins caused you this and that, and the words of this introduction continued until he completed them in the verse \"and you shall keep His statutes and His commandments that I command you this day, that it may go well with you and with your children after you, and that you may prolong your days upon the land that YHWH your Elohim gives you all the days\" (Deuteronomy 4:40). Then Moses called to all Israel who were before him and said (Deuteronomy 5:1): \"Hear, O Israel, the statutes and the judgments that I speak in your ears this day,\" and he began the exposition of the Torah with the Ten Commandments, so that they would hear them expounded from the mouth of the one who received them from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He; and afterward he made known to them the unity of the Name — \"Hear, O Israel, YHWH our Elohim, YHWH is one\" (Deuteronomy 6:4) — and all the commandments that are in this book. Therefore he explains here <b>\"that Moses spoke to all Israel\"</b> and there <b>\"And Moses called to all Israel\"</b>, for the exposition of the Torah and the completion of the commandments must take place in the assembly of all Israel, just as it was at the giving of the Torah. And because he prolonged the words of this introduction, Scripture returned to the place where it had left off at the beginning of the exposition of the Torah, and said (Deuteronomy 4:44–45): \"And this is the Torah that Moses set before the children of Israel; these are the testimonies, the statutes, and the judgments that Moses spoke to the children of Israel when they came out of Egypt,\" and he mentioned (Deuteronomy 4:46) that it was across the Jordan, in the valley, in the land of Sihon — which is the land of Moab — as is mentioned here, for Israel did not enter the land of Moab except in that portion that Sihon had conquered from them, as is explained in the book above (Numbers 21:13). Now, he mentioned two things here: he said that Moses spoke to the children of Israel \"according to all that YHWH commanded him to them\" — and this is a reference to the commandments that he would tell them in this book, which had not been mentioned until now in the Torah; and he said that they are \"according to all that HaShem commanded him\" — he did not add and did not subtract from what he had been commanded. And he mentioned this because he did not say of them <b>\"And YHWH spoke to Moses\"</b>; therefore he included them now, that they are all according to all that was commanded from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He. And he further said, \"Moses undertook (הוֹאִיל) to expound the Torah\" — and this is a reference to the commandments that were already stated, that he would return to expound them and to innovate matters in them. And the meaning of <b>\"Moses undertook (הוֹאִיל)\"</b> is that he wanted to expound the Torah to them, and he mentioned this to make known that on his own initiative he saw fit to do so, and HaShem did not command him in this — from the language of \"Please consent (הוֹאֶל נָא) and lodge\" (Judges 19:6), and \"If only we had consented (הוֹאַלְנוּ) and dwelt\" (Joshua 7:7), and so many others."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "In the phrase מִלַּת אֲמִירָה near the beginning, Ramban means that the word for the act of speaking is missing from the verse in Deut. 1:6; rendered literally as 'the word of saying/speaking.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:44:27.041316911Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}